RUN 1 (2026-09-11): the same, interrupted after alice's Claim - kept as it was recorded then
=============================================================================================================
The xmrswap COMMAND LINE as two separate processes, bob and alice: regtest monerod and monero-wallet-rpc, DERO simulator
=======================================================================================================================
Happy path, run on 2026-09-11 and INTERRUPTED before its end (below). Each party is its own OS process running the xmrswap binary built from the working tree, with its own
state file, its own DERO wallet (simulator 30006 bob, 30007 alice), its own monero-wallet-rpc (regtest :28084 bob,
:28083 alice) and one shared -channel directory. Machine paths are written <tmp>, <repo> and <user>.

How it was run, and what differs from a mainnet run
- -net regtest against the DERO simulator (the only DERO network -net regtest is accepted with). Not testnet or mainnet.
- -monerod 127.0.0.1:28081 on both: each companion names that monerod to its wallet-rpc after every open and generate.
- Logins from the environment only: VEIL_RPC_LOGIN and XMRSWAP_MONERO_LOGIN were set, and no login is on either
  command line. The simulator wallets and the regtest wallet-rpc were started without logins, so both companions warn
  (as they must off mainnet) that the wallets answered without credentials; on mainnet they would refuse to start.
- The channel is sealed (GAP-019): the maker's key came from `xmrswap channel-key`, and both processes read it from
  XMRSWAP_CHANNEL_KEY. Its value is written nowhere.
- The prover. The pinned proof-xmr is the Linux release build in WSL (sha256 4d3bde0a... = ProverSHA256). The command
  line has to run on Windows, because WSL here is in NAT mode and cannot reach the simulator, which listens on
  127.0.0.1 only; and -prover must be the absolute path of a binary the companion executes directly, which the Linux
  build is not, from Windows. So -prover names a scratch wrapper (proverwrap.exe, not in the repository) that hashes the
  WSL binary against the pin before every run and then runs it through wsl.exe with the same arguments and stdin, and
  the command line pins the wrapper with -prover-sha256, which it accepts off mainnet only and warns about. The
  command line's own pinned-prover check therefore ran against the wrapper's hash, not ProverSHA256; the proofs are the
  pinned build's.
- MT-B09: alice was given two -daemon, 127.0.0.1:20000 and localhost:20000 — the same simulator node under two names.
  That exercises the witness path (a second node read, network-checked and compared before the XMR lock); it is not
  an independent node, and the simulator has no second one.
- -htlc was given (the scid simhtlc installed for the reorganisation runs, pinned code). The default from the record
  (MT-A18) is shown separately below.
- Monero blocks: nothing mines on a regtest chain by itself, so the driver script mined one block about every 6 s.
- Policy: the command line's own defaults (t1 = t1-min + 20 = 420 blocks, t2 = t1 + 400, G = 100, 20 confirmations,
  stable 8, claim margin 8), with the stipend at its default. Nothing was lowered.

What the run did: INTERRUPTED, not finished
  Started 2026-09-11 20:17:17 AEST. The coordinator paused all work at about 20:23, and the pause killed this session's
  background shells; the two companions kept running, but from 20:24:59 (alice) and 20:27:18 (bob) every state save
  failed (see "The stall" below), and the driver script's 90-minute watchdog stopped both at 21:47:18. The DERO
  simulator was then wiped by a restart (it deletes its data directory on every start), so the DERO side of this run
  no longer exists. It is to be run again once the simulator is back.

  Contract   htlc.bas v2 at eecdd20779eabf7717462186938734e7ccaee51f8bd78dfda6f84466c6e2bfb5, given with -htlc.

  20:17:41  bob sent the offer over the sealed channel; 20:17:53 bob verified π_a, 20:17:57 alice verified π_b; both
